Plain backtick fence (no language):

<beginfold id='8'>```{foo}</beginfold id='8'>
raw text
<endfold id='8'>```</endfold id='8'>

Standard table:

| Column A | Column B | Column C |
|:---------|:--------:|---------:|
| left     | center   | right    |

Standard link: [text](https://example.com)

Colon directly after a link: [text](https://example.com): plain text, not part of the link (the underline Kate shows on links is view-layer, not syntax)

Reference link: [text][ref]

[ref]: https://example.com

Inline image: ![alt](image.png)

Wrapped link spanning lines:

Standard link: [text
more text](https://example.com)

Wrapped reference spanning lines:

Reference: [label
more text][ref]

Wrapped code span:

Use `inline code
spanning lines` here.

Back to normal text after the wrapped constructs.

Escaped backtick (must not open a wrapped span):

a \` literal backtick

Wrapped link with brackets and an image in the name:

[outer
[inner] group
more](https://example.com)

[see
![img](https://url/to/i.png)
more](https://example.com)

Stray bracket gives up (the ] ends the pending; the consumed text keeps
link styling, matching markdown's orphan-bracket behavior):

[text
more] x

Orphan same-line bracket (markdown parity):

[text] xxx

Line-initial emoji (ambiguous with field names; pinned as Field Name):

:joy: shipped!

:author: Jane Doe

Autolink: <https://example.com>

Email: <user@example.com>

Emphasis: *italic* and _also italic_

Strong: **bold** and __also bold__

Strikethrough: ~~deleted~~

Setext heading:

